# Untitled

## Engineering Homochiral Metal-Organic Frameworks for Heterogeneous Asymmetric Catalysis and Enantioselective Separation
Owing to the potential applications in technological areas such as gas storage, catalysis, separation, sensing and nonlinear optics, tremendous efforts have been devoted to the development of porous metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) over the past ten years. Homochiral porous MOFs are particularly attractive candidates as heterogeneous asymmetric catalysts and enantioselective adsorbents and separators for production of optically active organic compounds due to the lack of homochiral inorganic porous materials such as zeolites. In this review, we summarize the recent research progress in homochiral MOF materials, including their synthetic strategy, distinctive structural features and latest advances in asymmetric heterogeneous catalysis and enantioselective separation.
## 1. Introduction
Chirality has become increasingly significant in many fields such as pharmaceutics, chemical industry, agriculture and clinical analysis.[1] Even for enantiomers of the same substance, it has been proved to behave differently in the body or environment, or to be totally different metabolic products in the body being markers of different diseases. For example, usually one enantiomer of a pharmaceutical can be therapeutic, the other can be toxic.[2] Enantiomerically pure drugs are therefore being increasingly used in clinics. In 2004, five of the top 10 selling drugs that accounted for \(61\%\) of the global sale of pharmaceuticals were single enantiomer products.[3] The production and availability of enantiomerically pure compounds are thus of great importance to the fine chemical and pharmaceutical industries. Methods available to provide enantiopure chemical products include resolution of racemates, isolation from natural sources, fermentation and asymmetric catalysis, with resolution and catalysis still being used most often. A concise description of these methods has been given by Rehnske in 2001.[4]
In light of the importance of chirality, there have been tremendous research efforts devoted to the development of
porous materials such as chiral zeolites for producing optically pure fine chemicals via asymmetric catalysis and chiral separations over the past decades.[5–7] It has been found widespread applications in catalysis, ion-exchange, and separation for zeolites with regular nanoporous void space,[7,8] but no chiral zeolites are available in enantiopure form to date though there have been breakthroughs in the synthesis of zeolites with larger pores because of high-temperature calcination, which is necessary for removal of the surfactant templates to generate the porous architecture of the zeolite, inevitably destroys the chirality of the aluminosilicate–surfactant
assemblies.[8,9] The resulting achiral or racemic zeolites can not be explored for enantioselective separations and catalysis. This has prompted exploration of new strategies for the synthesis of homochiral porous materials that may find applications in enantioselective process.
Metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) also known as coordination polymers, are crystalline materials generated by the association of metal ions (nodes) and multitopic organic ligands (rods).[10–14] They have emerged as a very promising type of materials because of their unique attributes, open structures with periodic dual composition amenable to bottom-up assembly of secondary building blocks into a desired framework expanding and/or decorating a specific blueprint network topology.[15–19] Usually, MOFs are synthesized under very mild conditions, which should allow facile construction of homochiral MOFs by judicious choices of chiral building blocks or templates. Ideal homochiral MOFs can possess chiral functionalities that are accessible via the open channels or cavities. They may combine the advantages of both heterogeneous catalysts (e.g. facile separation and recovery of catalyst, high stability, and ease of handling) and their homogeneous analogues (e.g. homogeneity of active sites, high efficiency, reproducibility, selectivity, controllability, and mild reaction conditions). These features have allowed for MOFs bearing chiral functionalities that are accessible via the open channels or cavities and hence offer great potential to address diverse technical applications in chirotechnology.[21] As a result, fabrication of homochiral porous MOFs may expand the utility of zeolitic materials into the area of chirotechnology.
The application of MOFs as asymmetric heterogeneous catalysts has been covered in several significant reviews, but
a comprehensive overview of the research field on catalysis and separation is missing.[21–30] In this report, we present new advances in the syntheses of chiral MOF materials and illustrate the application of chiral heterogeneous catalysis and separation. Because of limited reports on chiral separation of MOFs, some typical examples based on metal-organic assemblies such as supramolecular porous materials that are not regarded as classic MOFs, are also included for comparison and discussion. The review will cover chiral MOFs with the case studies being organized according to synthetic strategy, structural feature, catalysis and separation.
## 2. Design and Synthesis
The synthetic methods reported can be classified into different groups according to their synthesis pathways.[21,22] There are three different well-established strategies for homochiral MOF materials:
(1). Direct synthesis: Synthesis of a homochiral MOF from either enantiopure ligands as cross-linking ligands or via self-resolution from totally achiral components to generate only one enantiomorph. (2). Chiral-template synthesis: Synthesis of homochiral MOFs from achiral building blocks in the presence of chiral molecules or charge-balanced ions that do not act as components ligands but organize building units into a particular topology around themselves in the crystallization progress by enantiospecific supramolecular interactions. (3). Post-synthesis: Synthesis of a homochiral MOF from a chiral or achiral parent MOF by synthetic modification of the organic struts or the metal nodes and by guest exchange.
## 2.1. Direct Synthesis
In theory, any MOF materials can crystallize in a chiral space group as a result of a particular spatial disposition of all the building units. The bulk samples of the MOFs built from achiral or racemic components via self-resolution however tend to contain both enantiomorphs and are thus racemic even though each single crystal is a single enantiomer. Moreover, it is difficult to systematically engineer such hybrid materials with desired chiral pores or functionalities to enhance the enantioselectivity of a separation or catalytic process. In spite of much effort, there are only several homochiral MOFs that have thus far been identified via self-resolution during crystal growth.[31] Currently, the most effective way to make crystalline homochiral MOFs is utilizing enantiopure organic linkers as cross-linking ligands or chiral co-ligands to control the stereochemistry at the metal centers. The direct participation of chiral components into the assembly process warrants the chirality in the resulting network structures.
In 1999, Aoyama et al. reported the first example of crystallizing homochiral MOF based on \(C\mathrm{d}^{2+}\) ions and achiral 5-(9-anthracenyl)pyrimidine (apd) bridging linker.[32] The chirality of the helical compound arises from a metal-ligand helical
