In 2007, Long and Zheng et al. demonstrated homochiral MOFs could be crystallized from achiral components by chemically manipulating the statistical fluctuation of enantiomeric pair formation.[34] This crystallization was accomplished by moderating the concentration of ammonia used as a competing ligand in the reaction mixture to control the kinetics of the polymer [Cu(succinate)(4,4'-bipyridine)]· \(4\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) (2). The homochiral Cu-based MOF can be grown by careful controlling of ammonia concentrations and solvent evaporation rates. The validity of this chiral symmetry breaking strategy that obviates the use of a seed crystal was further demonstrated by the preparation of two more coordination polymers \(\mathrm{[Cu(nitrilotriacetate)Na(H_{2}O)]_{n}]}\) and \(\mathrm{[Cu(glycolate)]_{n}]}\) in their respective homochiral forms.
MOF materials assembled from racemic organic ligands usually are conglomerates (racemic mixture of chiral crystals), but sometimes are not enantiomeric mixtures and have enantiomeric excesses. In 2009, Cui and coworkers reported six homochiral octupolar metal-organoboron frameworks \([\mathrm{M}_{2}\mathrm{L}_{1}(\mathrm{OH})(\mathrm{MeOH})]\cdot3\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) \((\mathrm{M}=\mathrm{Co}/\mathrm{Mn}/\mathrm{Ni}/\mathrm{Cu}/\mathrm{Zn}/\mathrm{Cd},\ 3\mathrm{a}-\mathrm{f})\), which were synthesized from racemic \(C_{3}\)-symmetric tris(4-benzoic acid)tridurylborane and the divalent metal ions under solvothermal conditions (Figure 1).[35] The six MOFs are isostructural and crystallize in the chiral cubic space group F432, and they adopt an eightfold-interpenetrating (10,3)-a network formed by linking bimetal building blocks with three bidentate carboxylate groups of bridging A-L ligands. Bulk crystals of each of the six compounds are not a racemic mixture, and their enantiomeric nature was demonstrated by solid-state circular dichroism spectra. Further study indicates that, although the synthesis begins from a racemic mixture of \(\Delta\)- and \(\Lambda\cdot\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{L}_{1}\), spontaneous resolution with simultaneous inversion of the absolute conformation around the boron of \(\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{L}_{1}\) occurs upon crystallization.
An increasing number of chiral MOFs have been built using commercially available chiral molecules as bridging ligands, including naturally occurring amino acids (such as asparagines, histidine and glutamic acid), hydroxyl carboxylic acids (such as lactic acid, malic acid, and tartaric acid), nucleotide acids, cinchona alkaloids, and biotin.[36–43] However, they are not always equipped with functional groups required for asymmetric catalysis and chiral separations, and usually the structural motifs in these frameworks are too dense to generate guest-accessible volume. Alternatively, homochiral MOFs can be made from appropriately designed chiral multifunctional organic linkers derived from privilege chiral ligands with known excellent asymmetric induction for target reactions or molecules.[22,44] Although more synthetically demanding, the approach is much more versatile and allows for the incorporation of a wide variety of catalysts.
Heterogenization of chiral molecular catalysts within porous MOFs has extensively studied by Lin, Kim and Hupp et al. over the past ten years.[19,21,28] In particularly, based on readily available chiral 1,1'-bi-2-naphthol, Lin and coworkers have designed a series of chiral pyridyl, carboxylate, and phosphonate bridging ligands with orthogonal functional groups.[21] These chiral bridging ligands are connected by metal nodes into 2D or 3D networks via their primary functional groups. The orthogonal secondary chiral functionalities that cannot link metal nodes periodically decorate the porous structures and can be utilized to generate asymmetric active sites that are accessible to the substrates via the open channels or cavities (See section 4).
Chiral oxazoline derivatives are efficient ligands to generate chiral coordination complexes, such as \(C_{2}\)-symmetric 2,6-bis(oxazolyl)pyridine, a chiral tecton has been used by Hosseini and coworkers in 2002 to generate a 1D chiral MOF \(\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{L}_{2})\mathrm{Cl}_{2}\cdot3\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{CN}\) (4) by coordinating to cobalt(II) ions.[45] The metal centers each adopts a distorted octahedral coordination geometry with the two apical positions occupied by two \(\mathrm{Cl}^{-}\)anions and the equatorial positions occupied by four \(\mathrm{N}\) atoms
of two consecutive tectons, one acting as a tripodal ligand and the other as a monodentate site. Due to the unsymmetrical and chiral nature of the ligand, the alignment of the 1D chains occurs in a parallel, rather than antiparallel fashion. Therefore, the molecular chirality is utilized to align molecular dipoles giving rise to a polar MOF.
In 2009, Cui and coworkers designed and synthesized a series of chiral \(^{1,1^{\prime}}\)-biphenyl derivative ligands bearing intrinsic \(C_{2}\) symmetry to construct a variety of homochiral MOFs. For example, three homochiral 3D MOFs \(\left[(\mathrm{AgL}_{3})\mathrm{NO}_{3}\right]\cdot i\cdot\mathrm{PrOH}\cdot8/3\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) (5), \(\left[\mathrm{Ag(L_{3})(PF_{6})_{1/6}(OH)_{5/6}}\right]\cdot3/2\mathrm{MeOH}\cdot\mathrm{CH}_{3}\cdot\mathrm{CN}\cdot3/2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) (6) and \(\left[\mathrm{Ag(L_{3})ClO_{4}}\right]\cdot3\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) (7) were assembled based on periodically ordered arrays of helices built from axial chiral \(^{3,3^{\prime}}\)-bipyridine-5,5',6,6'-tetramethyl-2,2'-dimethoxy-1,1'-biphenyl ligands and linearly coordinated \(\mathrm{Ag}\) (I) ions.[46] It is of significantly important that the cationic polymer skeleton of these three network exhibits an unprecedented conformational polymorphism in the solid-state, folding into two-, three- and four-fold helices with \(\mathrm{NO}_{3}^{-}\), \(\mathrm{PF}_{6}^{-}\)and \(\mathrm{ClO}_{4}^{-}\)as the counteranion, respectively (Figure 2). The \(2_{1}\) helices cross-link via argentophilic \(\mathrm{Ag-Ag}\) interactions to form sextuple helices, leading to a 3D chiral framework, and each \(3_{1}\) or \(4_{1}\) helices, on the other hand, self-associates in pairs to form three-dimensional tubular architectures. This anion-dependent self-assembly behavior can be rationalized by considering the sizes, geometries and binding abilities of the counteranions and subsequent chain conformation to minimize steric repulsions and maximize secondary interactions.
Despite decades of research, there are few examples of MOF materials in which an intrinsically chiral topology (e.g., quartz and zeolite) coexists with the chirality of enantiopure framework
building blocks. In 2008, Bu and coworkers described unusual integrated homochirality features in six 3D MOFs having enantiopure building blocks embedded in intrinsically chiral topological nets, of which quartz net is the most well known.[47] The materials were prepared from \(D\)- or L-camphoric acid and trivalent or divalent metal ions in the presence of achiral template cations or molecules under solvothermal conditions. Single-crystal analysis showed that all six compounds have three homochiral features: 3-D intrinsically homochiral net (quartz, quartz dual or srs net), homohelicity, and enantiopure molecular chirality (Figure 3). Moreover, the absolute helicity in each case can be controlled by the chirality of molecular building blocks.
An enantiopure co-ligand can be used to direct homochiral crystallization of an intrinsically chiral MOF based on achiral building units. As described above, in the absence of enantiopure co-ligands, the bulk product of MOFs built from achiral building blocks tends to be racemic. The validity of this approach was first demonstrated by Rosseinsky et al. in 2000 by generating a homochiral porous MOF of the type \(\left[\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{btc})_{2}(\mathrm{py})_{6}(1,2\mathrm{-pd})\right]\) \((\mathrm{btc}=1,3,5\)-benzenetricarboxylate) using enantiopure 1,2-propanediol \((1,2\mathrm{-pd})\) as co-ligands (see section 5 for details).[48] Subsequently, Inoue et al. reported the use of enantiopure 1,2-diaminopropane \((1,2\mathrm{-pn})\) as co-ligands to direct the formation of a homochiral network of \(\left[\left\{\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{CN})_{6}\right\}\left\{\mathrm{Mn}(S)\right\}\right.\) \(\left.\mathrm{pH}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})\right\}]\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\right)\) (8).[49] A bimetallic square formed by four cyanide groups in the \(\left[\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{CN})_{6}\right]^{3-}\) and \(\mathrm{Mn}^{2+}\) is arranged almost perpendicular to the \(c\) axis. In each unit, there are one chiral (S)-pn ligand and two water molecules between the sheets, with the ligand and one of the water molecules coordinating to an \(\mathrm{Mn}^{2+}\) ion and thus separating the sheets. In this case, the
coordinating (S)-pnH acts as a chiral chelate ligand that not only controls the stereochemistry of \(\mathrm{Mn}^{2+}\) ions but also regulates the crystal growth to afford homochiral layered networks.
## 2.2. Chiral-Template Synthesis
Template, that is most generally defined as a structure directing agent,[50] could be charged organic ions, chiral solvents and even coordination complexes. The structure and chemical properties of guest template molecules can be transcribed into pore shapes and properties. The use of charge-compensating organic templates (also called structure-directing agents, most commonly tetraalkylammonium cations or protonated cationic amines) to direct the self-assembly of molecular precursors into extended frameworks is widely studied in inorganic porous materials.[51] Some porous MOFs with positive-charged organic templates are also known, but few of them contain chiral templates.[52]
The enantiospecific supramolecular interactions between the cationic counterparts and anionic networks have been used to prepare bimetallic oxalate-based networks. In 2004, Gruselle and coworkers demonstrated that ferrocenyl substituted ammonium ions acted as efficient template cations of 2D oxalate-based networks.[53] The planar chiral 1-(N,N,N-tri(alkyl) aminomethyl)-2-methylferrocene \([1-\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{NR}_{3}-2-\mathrm{CH}_{3}-\mathrm{C}_{5}\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{Fe}-\mathrm{C}_{5}\mathrm{H}_{5}]^{+}\) \(\mathbf{R}=\mathbf{C}\mathbf{H}_{3}\), \(n\mathrm{-}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{5}\), \(n\mathrm{-}\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{H}_{7}\), \(n\mathrm{-}\mathrm{C}_{4}\mathrm{H}_{9}\)) cations, associated with \(D_{3}\) \(\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{oxalato})_{3}^{3-}\) anions form diastereostereomeric ion pairs in solution. Their chiral iodide salts were thus tentatively used to template the enantioselective formation of 2D Mn-based networks starting from racemic \([\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{oxalato})_{3}]^{3-}\). In 2008,
Train and Gruselle et al. prepared a 2D honeycomb network 9 of \([\mathrm{MnCr}(\mathrm{oxalato})_{3}]^{3-}\) in its two enantiomeric forms by enantioselective self-assembly under the control of a tetraalkylammonium cation \([\mathrm{N}(\mathrm{CH}_{3})(\mathrm{n}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{H}_{7})_{2}(\mathrm{s}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{C}_{4}\mathrm{H}_{9})]^{+}\) template through a specific interactions between the chiral alkyl chain of the template cation and the tris(oxalato)chromate (III) moieties.[54] The chiral carbon atom on one chain of the template is enough to twist the corresponding oxalate group and impose the configuration of the chromium centers. The above results clearly establish that the existence of enantiospecific interactions can be exploited to obtain optically active compounds using chiral counter cations as templating agents
A chiral induction effect in the ionothermal synthesis was reported by Morris and coworkers in 2007, to direct spontaneous crystallization of homochiral MOFs of the same enantiomorph.[55] The use of l-aspartate as one component of the ionic liquid 1-butyl-3-methylimidazolium (BMIm)-l-aspartate induces homochirality in the resulting solid \((\mathrm{BMIm})_{2}[\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{btc}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{H})_{2}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{2}]\) (10), though the anion is not occluded by the material and the solid itself is built from only nonchiral building blocks (Figure 4).
The enantiomeric nature was confirmed by single-crystal X-ray analysis of ten randomly selected crystals. It's a chiral template-directed course further supported by the evidence that D-aspartate included ionic liquid led to a crystallized material with the opposite chirality.
Bu et al. has subsequently extended the concept of chiral influence to amplify the chiral induction of homochiral MOFs from achiral precursors by employing substoichiometric amounts of a chiral spectator. In the presence of small amounts of the nature alkaloids \((-)\)-cinchonidine or \((+)\)-cinchonine, homochiral MOFs \((\mathrm{Me}_{2}\mathrm{NH}_{2})[\mathrm{In}(\mathrm{thb})_{2}]\cdot\mathrm{xDMF}\) (11) were crystallized from achiral building blocks \((\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{thb}\) refers to thiophenen-2,5-dicarboxylic acid).[56] MOF materials of opposite chirality could be obtained when they were prepared in the presence of \((-)\)-cinchonidine or \((+)\)-cinchonine. In the absence of chiral alkaloid, the resulting products were found to be racemic twins, further demonstrating the chirality induction effect of natural alkaloids.
In addition to the zeolite-like templating method by protonated cationic amines, In 2007, Bu and coworkers demonstrated a rare example of metal–organic frameworks templated by an enantiopure anionic template.[57] The homochiral MOF \([\mathrm{Cu}_{2}(4,4^{\prime}\mathrm{-bipy})]\cdot(\mathrm{D}\mathrm{-Hcam})_{2}\cdot(4,4^{\prime}\mathrm{-bipy})_{2}\cdot12\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) (12) were hydrothermally synthesized from a ligand mixture of 4,4-bipyridine and D-camphrolic acids and copper(II) nitrates (Figure 5). The \(4,4^{\prime}\)-bipy ligands bridge tetrahedral \(\mathrm{Cu}^{+}\)ions to form a twofold interpenetrating diamond framework. The positively charged framework is charge balanced by guest D-Hcam anions.
The chiral template approach was also extended to synthesis of chiral polyoxometalates-based materials. For example, in 2006,
Wang and Su et al. reported the preparation of \(\mathrm{KH}_{2}[(\mathrm{proline})_{4}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})\) \(\mathrm{Cu}_{3}][\mathrm{W}_{12}\mathrm{O}_{40}]\cdot\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) (13) by utilizing enantiopure copper-proline complexes as templates and Keggin-type \([\mathrm{BW}_{12}\mathrm{O}_{40}]^{5-}\) polyoxoanions as building blocks under hydrothermal conditions.[58] The CD spectra of compounds made from \(D\cdot\) and \(L\) -proline reveal significant induced optical activity in the polyoxometalate moieties, indicating that the templates can transfer the chirality of organic ligands to the whole framework through the interaction between metal centers and hybrid frameworks.
## 2.3. Post-Synthesis
When self-assembly fails for the synthesis of MOFs with functional linkers, the post-synthetic modification of a parent MOF
appears to be a very valuable alternative.[59] The construction of MOF networks is very sensitive to the chemical reactivity and solubility of functionalized linkers. This is particularly the case for functions such as \(-\mathrm{OH}\), \(-\mathrm{NH}_{2}\), \(-\mathrm{COOH}\), and N-donating groups, which can interfere with the coordination chemistry associated with the assembly of the secondary building blocks, thereby leading to undesirable compounds.[60,61]
Recent studies demonstrated that targeting their organic units may allow MOFs to be readily modified in a post-synthetic manner with a variety of covalent transformations.[19,59–61] Postsynthesis allows for the fabrication of homochiral porous MOFs from either homochiral porous MOFs or achiral porous MOFs. Lin and coworkers have systematically discussed post-synthetic modification of chiral porous MOFs to generate heterogeneous asymmetric catalysts (see section 4).[21]
In 2005, Mirkin and coworkers demonstrated that ion exchange can act as a way of tuning or controlling the chemical compositions of chiral MOFs.[62] Polymer particles based on \(\mathrm{Zn}^{2+}\) ions and homochiral carboxylate-functionalized bis-Zn-tridentate Schiff base building blocks (BMSB) can be successfully exchanged with \(\mathrm{Cu(II)}\) by stirring a solution of dispersed particles with copper acetate at room temperature, resulting in particles with \(\mathrm{Cu(II)}\) ions incorporated into the ligand pockets. During the ion exchange process, the particles retain their shape and size. Note that exchange does not occur at the carboxylate nodes. This type of exchange process works for other salts, including \(\mathrm{Mn(II)}\), \(\mathrm{Pd(II)}\) and \(\mathrm{Ni(II)}\), although in some cases one needs to use an excess of metal ion to effect complete exchange (interior and exterior sites). However, their fluorescence is quenched due to the \(\mathrm{Cu(II)}\) ions, which replace the \(\mathrm{Zn(II)}\) ions coordinated to the
tridentate Schiff base ligands. This metathesis strategy was further advanced by Kim et al. in 2009, who demonstrated that the complete and reversible exchange of metal ions constituting a robust microporous MOF while maintaining not only the structural integrity of the framework but also single crystallinity.[68]
In 2009, Kim and coworkers demonstrated for the first time that chiral porous MOF materials could be fabricated from preassembled achiral frameworks by post-synthetic modification with chiral catalytic units.[64] In sharp contrast to unmodified parent MIL-101, these two chiral MOFs are capable of catalyzing asymmetric aldol reactions with high enantioselectivity, though they have similar framework structures with MIL-101 (see section 4).
In a related report, Cohen and coworkers shown that IRMOF- \(3^{[65]}\) can also react with chiral anhydrides, such as (R)-2- methylbutanoic anhydride to modify IRMOF-3 quantitatively, converting an achiral MOF to a chiral MOF material (Figure 6a). Preliminary results suggested that a mixture of both open-form amide and closed-form imide products were obtained when IRMOF-3 was treated with (S)-(-)-2-acetoxysuccinic anhydride under similar conditions. This further demonstrates the feasibility and efficiency of generating chiral MOFs from achiral using the post-synthesis approach.[66]
In 2009, Bauer and coworkers reported the post-synthesis of a 3D porous framework \([Z\mathrm{n}_{4}\mathrm{O}(m e s o{-}4,4^{\prime}{-}(1,2\) -dibromoethane1,2-diyl)dibenzoate)3] (15) by modifying \([Z\mathrm{n}_{4}\mathrm{O}(t r a n s{-}4,4^{\prime}\) -stilbenedicarboxylate)3] (14) with \(\mathrm{Br}_{2}\) in \(\mathrm{CHCl}_{3}\) or \(\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}\) in the dark, as a result of diastereoselective bromination of the stilbene units (Figure 6b).[67] The resulting stereocontrol originates in the rigidity of the coordinated organic linker and the porous structure of the MOF, allowing "solution-like" access to reactive sites. The stability of this interpenetrated MOF structure could be increased by the partial bromination, demonstrated by X-ray diffraction and \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) sorption studies. This study further suggests
that the post-synthesis strategy of suitable achiral MOFs could be employed as a method for stereoselective or enantioselective synthesis.
## 3. Distinctive Structural Features
High porosity and high surface area, together with permanent pore size and pore shape, are the merits of homochiral MOFs and are currently considered to be key factors for their application in catalysis and separation.[68] Large open channels are necessary for asymmetric catalytic reactions and enantioselective separations because of the need to transport large organic substrates and/or products. MOF materials can be categorized into robust and flexible species on the basis of the structural transformation ability.[68–70] Despite relying on weaker bonds than zeolites, MOFs have demonstrated permanent microporosity and do not show any
structural transformations upon applying external stimuli such as guest molecule, heat, or magnetic or electric field. Other MOFs have shown flexible behaviors, a property not typically observed with metal oxides, in that they can convert between different structures via external stimuli. Those chiral MOFs exhibiting dynamic behavior may be developed into unique classes of materials, such as highly selective catalytic and separating materials, which could not be obtained with rigid porous materials.[22,30]
## 3.1. Porosity with Robustness
Crystallinity and robustness are both desirable for MOFs but not necessarily easily reconciled at the same time. To compensate for the energetic penalty of forming a pore, a framework solid must be robust and sustained by sufficiently strong interactions. Usually, solids assembled by such strong interactions precipitate rapidly and lack long-range order, that's why stability and hence porosity often are viewed as competing features with crystallinity. A guideline for rigid pores in MOFs is the use of stiff building units linked with strong chemical bonds such as coordination and/or covalent bond to form a 3D framework.[12] Despite of much effort, most homochiral MOFs reported are not robust enough to show permanent porosity owing to the contradiction between large pores and low stability of the network, nor porous enough to be useful for selective sorption or catalytic transformation of organic molecules.
As the search for robust and porous MOFs continues, much attention is steered toward synthesizing materials based on elaborately designed rigid and elongated bridging ligands. In 2008, Lin and coworkers reported the synthesis of a BINOL-derived tetracarboxylate bridging ligand to build homochiral MOFs in combination with copper paddle-wheel building units under acidic conditions.[71] Treating copper(II) salts with
\((R)\mathrm{-H_{4}L_{4}}\) in diethylformamide (DEF) at elevated temperature afforded the compound 16 \([\mathrm{Cu}_{2}(R\mathrm{-}\mathrm{L}_{4})(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{2}]\cdot12\mathrm{DEF}\cdot16\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O},\) which possesses large channels of \(3.2\ \mathrm{nm}\) along the \(a/b\) -axis and represents the first example of a mesoporous homochiral MOF (Figure 7). Interestingly, when the crystals were grown in dimethylformamide (DMF), the enantiopure ligand \(\mathbf{L}_{4}\) gave a non-interpenetrating structure, while the racemic \(\mathbf{L}_{4}\) yielded a 2-fold interpenetrated 17 -meso- \(\mathrm{[L_{4}C u_{2}(H_{2}O)_{2}]\cdot8D M F\cdot4H_{2}O}\) with both the copper paddle wheels and the \(\mathbf{L}_{4}\) ligands serving as 4-connected nodes (Figure 7). Both enantiopure and racemic \(\mathbf{L}_{4}\) yielded non-interpenetrating structures when the crystals were grown from diethylformamide (DEF). This system thus exhibits remarkable cationation isomerism that is controlled by both chirality of the bridging ligand and the molecular size of solvents used to grow crystals. Due to the very open structures, the frameworks severely distorted when the solvent molecules were removed, as evidenced by the loss of PXRD peaks, and exhibited surface areas much smaller than those calculated from single crystal data. Despite of such evacuation induced distortion, experiment studies on their adsorption of Brilliant Blue R-250 demonstrated the maintenance of open channels in these frameworks and the accessibility of the open channels to nanoscale molecules in solution. Obviously, as the ligand length increases, the resulting MOFs experience severe framework distortion upon solvent removal or interpenetration of multiple networks, which significantly reduces the porosity and negatively impacts the guest uptake capacity.
In 2009, Lin and coworkers figured out a way of rigidifying frameworks against distortions by unusual interlocking and interpenetration in the above \(\mathrm{{Cu}}^{2+}\) and \(\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{L}_{4}\) system.[72] Prolonged heating of \((R){\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{L}_{4}\) and \({\mathrm{Cu}}({\mathrm{NO}}_{3})_{2}\) under basic conditions (containing ammonia) led to the formation of \((R){\mathrm{-}}[\mathrm{L}_{4}\mathrm{Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{DMF})_{2}][(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{L}_{4})\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})]\cdot13\mathrm{DMF}\cdot10\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) (18). The same reaction carried out with racemic \(\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{L}_{4}\) gave \([\{R\mathrm{-}\mathrm{L}_{4}\}\mathrm{Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{DMF})_{2}[S\mathrm{-}\mathrm{L}_{4}]\mathrm{Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{DMF})(\mu\mathrm{-}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{0.5}]\cdot14\mathrm{DMF}\cdot9\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) (19) (Figure 8). Single crystal X-ray diffraction study of 18 revealed a 3D \((\mathrm{R}){-}[\mathrm{L}_{4}\mathrm{Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{DMF})_{2}]\) network that is interlocked with a parallel array of 2D grids formed by the Cu centers and \((R){\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{L}\) ligands. The (4,4)-connected 3D \((R){\mathrm{-}}[\mathrm{L}_{4}\mathrm{Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{DMF})_{2}]\) network in 18 formed by Cu centers and R-L ligands is essentially the same as the \((R){\mathrm{-}}[\mathrm{L}_{4}\mathrm{Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{2}]\) framework in 16, with the exception of coordination to DMF instead of water in the axial
positions. It is in stark contrast with those synthesized from the same building blocks under acidic conditions for the structures of 18 and 19, thus illustrating the subtle dependence of MOF framework structures on the synthetic conditions. The PXRD patterns of the evacuated samples are essentially the same as those of the pristine crystals, indicating the maintenance of the framework structure upon solvent removal. The interlocking and interpenetration have significantly rigidified the frameworks of these new MOFs to endow them with high permanent porosity and framework stability.
In 2009, Lin et al. reported another strategy to rigidify the frameworks for highly porous and robust by using high connectivity of aromatics-rich octacarboxylic acid bridging ligand \(\mathrm{H}_{8}\mathrm{L}_{5}\).[73] Treating the octacarboxylic acids with \({\mathrm{Cu}}(\mathrm{II})\) salts in diethylformamide or dimethylformamide led to three isostructural MOFs \([\mathrm{Cu}_{4}(\mathrm{L}_{5})(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{4}]\) -xguest (20a–c). They are isostructural and adopt the known but very rare (4,8)-connected scu topology, with the copper paddle wheels serving as 4-connected nodes and the \(\mathbf{L}_{5}\) acting as 8-connected nodes (Figure 9). The experimental surface areas of \(\mathbf{20a-c}\) almost agree with those calculated values. The PXRD patterns of the evacuated samples are essentially the same as those of the pristine ones, further demonstrating the maintenance of the framework structure upon solvent removal. The higher connectivity of the octacarboxylic acid ligands could apparently stabilized these frameworks against distortion, distinctly different from the above results which showed that the 4,4-connected MOFs based on elongated tetracarboxylate bridging ligands severely distorted
to give experimental surface areas only a very small fraction of those calculated.
## 3.2. Porosity with Flexibility
As described above, the flexible MOFs are interesting because of the spontaneous arrangement of structure into the appropriate spatial orientation, which might be rearranged by a balance of attractive and repulsive forces between host and guest, happened to them as responding to the external stimuli. It's key principle for high selectivity recognition, accommodation, and separation of a target molecule. The flexibility is generally created by (1) the flexible ligands employed in constructing frameworks, (2) the weaker interactions such as coordination bonds, H-bonds, \(\pi\)-electron stacking and van der Waals interactions.[69,74] The framework flexibility thus may come from the rotation, or expansion/contraction of ligand. For interpenetrated and interdigitated frameworks, the dynamics can arise from the slipping motion of the interpenetrated layers even if the singular framework is rigid, when the interaction between frameworks, degree of interpenetration and their topology strongly affect the flexibility.
A guideline for flexible pores in MOFs is the use of flexible building units linked with strong bonds, stiff building blocks connected with weaker bonds, or flexible building blocks linked with weaker bonds.[69] Structural shifting should disfavor stability, and therefore porosity, and unquestionably, crystallinity should be diminished by movement of components in the solid. Though some exceptions do exist, solids that have been reported to harmonize dynamics and crystallinity typically have components sustained by non-covalent interactions partially or completely, as the energetic minima in the conversion profile would be shallower.
In 2005, Lin and coworkers reported a homochiral porous MOF \(\left[\mathrm{Cd}\left(\mathrm{I}_{6}\right)_{2}\left(\mathrm{ClO}_{4}\right)_{2}\right] \cdot 11\mathrm{EtOH} \cdot 6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) \(\left[\mathrm{I}_{6}=\right.\)
(S)-2,2'-diethoxy-1,1'-bimaphthyl-6,6'-bis(4-vinylpyridine)] (21), based on 1D polymeric chains built from the elongated di-pyridyl ligand \(\mathrm{I}_{6}\) and \(\mathrm{Cd}^{2+}\) ions.[75] All 1D chains lie parallel to each other in the \(ab\) plane due to interdigitation. The resultant layer structures are further stabilized by strong \(\pi-\pi\) stacking interactions to generate a porous structure. Once the included solvent molecules were removed out, the pristine crystals lost crystallinity, as shown by PXRD. However, when the obtained amorphous material was exposed to a solvent vapor, the crystallinity could be readily restored. Of particular interesting is that the framework could undergo interesting reversible single-crystal to single-crystal structural transformations induced by ethanol and benzene guest exchange. This study indicates that the framework integrity of homochiral porous MOF could be maintained under typical heterogeneous conditions with a solvent.
In 2007, Bu and coworkers reported five new homochiral MOFs 22-26 synthesized from D-camphoric acid \(\left(\mathrm{D}-\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{cam}\right)\) and
\(4,4^{\prime}\)-trimethylenedipyridine (tmdpy).[76] In all structures, each \(\mathrm{M}^{2+}\) is four-connected, bonded to two tmdpy ligands and two D-cam ligands, and each D-cam ligand bridges two \(\mathrm{M}^{2+}\) sites (similar to dicoordinate oxygen sites in zeolites) to form a \(2_{1}\) helix along the \(c/b\)-axis. These materials thus adopt either threefold or fourfold interpenetrating homochiral diamond nets. Interestingly, the flexibility of helices allows the single crystal to single crystal transformation from \(\left[\mathrm{Cd}\left(\mathrm{D}-\mathrm{cam}\right)\right.\) (tmdpy)] \(\cdot 2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) (22) to anhydrous \(c\)-\(\left[\mathrm{Cd}\left(\mathrm{D}-\mathrm{cam}\right)\right.\) (tmdpy)] (23) at \(100\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) and subsequent polymorphic displacive phase transition into \(\beta\)-\(\left[\mathrm{Cd}\left(\mathrm{D}-\mathrm{cam}\right)\right.\) (tmdpy)] (24) at \(140\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) (Figure 10). Both homochiral \(\beta\)-\(\left[\mathrm{Cd}\left(\mathrm{D}-\mathrm{cam}\right)\right.\) (tmdpy)] (24) and \(\gamma\left[\mathrm{Cd}\left(\mathrm{D}-\mathrm{cam}\right)\right.\) (tmdpy)] (25) have the same topological type, whereas contain lattice interpenetration of a different order, meanwhile, it's unusual that the chirality of the 3D framework can be readily switched between chiral \(\left(\gamma\left[\mathrm{Cd}\left(\mathrm{D}-\mathrm{cam}\right)\right.\right.\) (tmdpy)] (25) and achiral forms with negligible changes in all six unit cell parameters. As a result, the whole chiral framework is flexible associated with the compression or stretching of the helices, thereby endowing the single-crystal to single-crystal transformation from hydrated phase to dehydrated phase and displacive phase transition between different dehydrated phases.
The structural variations in MOFs related to the flexibility of the helices were also reported by Cui and coworkers.[77] In 2009, they built a porous 3D material 27 \(\left[\mathrm{ZnL}_{7}\right]\)-guest \(\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{L}_{7}=\left(\mathrm{R},\mathrm{R}\right) \cdot 1\right)\) \(\left(\mathrm{N},\mathrm{N}^{\prime}\right.\)-bis(3-tert-butyl-5-(4-ethynylpyridyl)salicylidene)-1,2-diaminocyclohexane) using a flexible 1D polymer made from semiflexible pyridyl-functionalized salen-based ligand. Each \(\mathrm{ZnL}_{7}\) unit uses one terminal pyridyl group to coordinate another \(\mathrm{ZnL}_{7}\) unit building an infinite zigzag chain. Strong interchain \(\pi \ldots \pi\) interactions allow direct packing of 1D strands in parallel into a 2D neutral rhombohedral grid, which stack on top of each other in a staggered arrangement along the \(a\) axis forming a 3D
porous supramolecular structure. The resulting structure bends and twists when its polymer chains stretch, triggered by guest molecules – the aromatic compounds - entering or leaving the structure (Figure 11). The single-crystal to the single-crystal structural transformation changes the cross-sectional shape of the larger channel from a parallelogram in \(\left[\mathrm{ZnL}_{7}\right]\) - benzene to a pseudosquare in \(\left[\mathrm{ZnL}_{7}\right]\) -toluene. The PXRD pattern of the evacuated sample is the same as that of the pristine one, showing the maintenance of the porous structure upon guest removal. The driving force to tune the shapes and sizes of the two kinds of channels might be the presence of a higher affinity \(\mathrm{CH}{-}\pi\) and hydrophobic interactions between the pore surfaces and aromatic guest molecules. The structure's flexibility and the host-guest interactions within its hydrophobic channels allow it to bind to and separate aromatic hydrocarbons from aliphatic mixtures.
## 3.3. Biomimetics
Enzymes generally display three major types of selective advantages: chemoselectivity, regioselectivity /diastereoselectivity and enantioselectivity,[78] which, and especially the latter, are the major reasons why synthetic chemists have become interested
in biocatalysis. The performances of some MOF materials may reassemble those of enzymes if they can combine isolated metal cluster, dynamic guest–host responses and tailorable pore surfaces.
Basically, MOFs with the large void volume and high surface area are the main target in some applications. However, an efficiency of some applications for instance, chiral catalysis and separation, the efficiency directly depends on the communication between host framework and guest molecules.[21,30,69] The pore surfaces of MOFs can be readily tailored by modifying the organic ligand through \(\pi{-}\pi\) interaction, hydrogen bonding, and electron donor/acceptor functionalities. The guestresponsive pores of flexible MOFs provide strong confinement effects for specific guest molecules by shrinking or expanding in
different guest-inclusion states. As a consequence, any type of chirality present in the substrate molecule is "recognized" upon the formation of the MOF-substrate complex, consequently, a prochiral substrate may be transformed into an optically active product and both enantiomers of a racemic substrate may react at different rates, inducing extensive and highly selective recognition and inclusion abilities.[21,30]
In principle, generating bio-analogous MOFs may be realized by using bioactive organic ligands such as amino acids, hydroxyl carboxylic acids, nucleotide acids, cinchona alkaloids, peptides and biotin.[36–40] These bioactive species are the origin for the functional
properties and highly selective substrate-binding ability of many extended biological structures, and so are attractive options as chiral building blocks for such purposes. Another option is the combination of flexible organic linkers with rich hydrophobic and/or hydrophilic groups with coordination bonds.[79]
Peptides are attractive ligands for the design of metal organic frameworks, with the potential to confer chirality and biological activity on these materials. In 2008, Taubert and Manton et al. reported the first example of metal peptide frameworks (MPFs) 28, also termed "bioinspired" analogues to the well-known MOFs, assembled from an oligovaline peptide Z-(L\(\mathrm{Val})_{2}\)-L-Glu(OH)-OH through metal-oxygen and metal-nitrogen bonding and various types of hydrogen bonding along with \(\pi...\pi\) stacking (Figure 12).[50] The structure of MPF 28 can be described in terms of two 2D substructures interconnected via hydrogen bonding, i.e. the first is a double layer of copper ions in the \(ab\) plane that are diagonally displaced with respect to one another, and the second double layer in the \(ab\) plane is that of the peptide with its long molecule axis oriented roughly along [001]. Besides hydrogen bonding between the peptides, the phenyl rings form a column along [010], which is stabilized by \(\pi...\pi\) stacking. Interestingly, there is a \(\beta\)-sheet-like structure and hydrogen bonding in the peptide backbone of this MPF.
Moreover, the crystal structure further shows that not all of the peptide is part of the \(\beta\)-sheet, and only the carbamate and the L-valine-L-valine peptide bond are involved. To accommodate the rather large side chains of the valine units through maximizing the number of hydrogen bonds, the \(\beta\)-sheet is distorted slightly, which enables the L-glutamic acid residue to adapt to the metal center coordination geometry. The crystallographic results thus reveal a more complex bonding scheme than simple \(\beta\)-sheets and also shows that the \(\beta\)-sheet orientation is parallel and not antiparallel along the \(b\)-axis. The metal-peptide architecture is stable up to \(\sim250\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) and has some internal porosity, which makes it a promising prototype for the further development of bio-MOFs and holds great potential applications in enantioselective processes.
In 2008, Marsh and coworkers describe four bio-MOFs 29-32 formed by the complexation of di- and tripeptides with \(Cd^{2+}\) ions (Figure 13).[81] The dipeptide complexes \(\mathrm{Cd}(\mathrm{L-Ala}_{2})_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{Cd}(\mathrm{L-Ala},\mathrm{L-Thr})_{2}\cdot4\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) form covalently linked 2D square lattices. The tripeptide complex of cadmium with glycine, \(\mathrm{Cd}(\mathrm{Gly})_{3}\cdot\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\), forms a 2D complex in which the carboxylate group of the peptide bridges between \(Cd^{2+}\) ions, while \(\mathrm{Cd}(\mathrm{L-Ala}_{3})_{2}\) from the tripeptide complex of cadmium with alanine forms a 1D extended molecular chain comprised of an infinite series of rings linked together through the Cd ions. It was found that hydrogen bonding between peptide amide groups and hydrophobic interactions between side chains plays significant roles in defining the infinite structures. Substitution of the more hydrophilic threonine side chain in place of alanine introduces a layer of water molecules into the crystal lattice.
Adenine, a purine nucleobase, is ideal for constructing porous biomolecular assemblies because of its rigid framework and diverse possible coordination sites. In 2004, Castillo and Luque et al. reported the first 3D MOF containing a nucleobase as a bridging ligand, \([\mathrm{Cu}_{2}(\mu\mathrm{-}\mathrm{ade})_{4}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{2}][\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{ox})(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})]_{2}\cdot14\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) (33), which was synthesized by reaction of adenine (Hade) with a basic solution of copper oxalate.[82] The compound has a covalent 3D network, in which the copper(II) centers are bridged by tridentate \(\mu\)-N3,N7,N9 adeninate ligands, and has relatively large, nanometer-sized tubes associated with the self-assembly process directed solely by metal–ligand interactions. Upon removing of the guest water molecules trapped in the nanotubes, the framework remains intact. In 2009, Rosi et al. reported a permanently porous Zn-adeninate MOF \(Z\mathrm{n}_{8}(\mathrm{adeni}\) nate)4(biphenyldicarboxylate)6·2Me2NH2·8DMF·11H2O (34), obtained by the reaction between adenine and zinc salt under the help of auxiliary linking molecule biphenyldicarboxylic
acid.[83] This compound has infinite Zn-adeninate columns composed of apex-sharing zinc-adeninate octahedral cages, with each cage containing four adeninates and eight \(Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}\) tetrahedra. The 1D Zn-adeninate columns are linked by multiple biphenyldicarboxylate to a 3D porous framework. Remarkably, the whole framework is anionic, and dimethylammonium cations as well as DMF and water guests reside in the channels. The intrinsic anionic and porous nature of this bio-MOF is demonstrated by the fact that it can serve as a host for adsorbing cationic drug molecule. It is worthy noting that adenine has multiple Lewis basic sites, including an amino group and pyrimidine nitrogens, and so the adenine-based chiral MOF may find a variety of applications in enantioselective catalysis and separation.
Due to their availability and ease of functionalization, sugar derivatives are attractive candidates as chiral building blocks for potentially useful homochiral MOFs. In 2003, Robson and coworkers prepared a 3D zinc D-saccharate MOF 35 from an aqueous solution containing potassium hydrogen saccharate and zinc acetate.[84] The compound has a four-connected 3D porous framework, with each Zn center being attached to four ligands and each ligand being attached to four zinc centers. There are two types of isolated channels with different characteristics: one hydrophilic and the other hydrophobic, in the robust network, both of which run parallel to the tetragonal axis with roughly square cross section. The zinc saccharate framework possessing two quite different channels within the crystal lattice may realize the isolation and storage of mixtures and control reaction between two or more molecules.
With the aim of generating enzyme like metal-organic assemblies, the use of flexible organic linkers with rich hydrophobic and/or hydrophilic groups is emerging. For example, Cui and coworkers have recently focused on chiral metal Schiff base complexes, which have been widely applied in asymmetric reactions and recognitions as excellent chiral scaffolds and can be readily designed and modified to be biomimetic in character. They developed a well-defined lamellar supramolecular host system based on tridentate Schiff base metal complexes that features a flexible helical framework and amphiphilic channel surfaces.[79] The amphiphilic channel surfaces provided the porous polymeric networks with strong affinities for amphiphilic guest molecules and could be used in enantioselective separation of racemic alcohols (see section 5).
## 4. Heterogeneous Asymmetric Catalysis
The use of MOFs as solid catalysts is particularly interesting because the pore size and functionality of the framework can be adjusted over a wide range for a variety of catalytic reactions. However, most documented homochiral MOFs have not proven sufficiently robust to exhibit permanent porosity, making this topic a continuing challenge.[21,26,28] Meanwhile, because of difficulties associated with the generation of catalyst active sites, only a handful of successful examples have thus far been reported. Indeed, MOF-based asymmetric catalysis is still in its infancy, despite the great importance and major practical advantages such as reduced contamination in products caused by catalytic residues, simple and safe procedure, facile recovery and reuse of the chiral catalysts together with some special
utilities vs traditional heterogeneous catalysts and so on.[24–26] The reported catalyses can be basically divided into two categories: organic catalysis and metal catalysis, according to the different catalytic sites in the porous network as well as distinct mechanisms of the reactions.
## 4.1. Organic Catalysis
In 2000, Kim and coworkers reported the first example of enantioselective catalysis with homochiral MOFs,[19] although coordination networks used as Lewis acids for heterogeneous catalysis have been known for several years prior to this. Homochiral \(\left[\mathrm{Zn}_{3}(\mu_{3}-\mathrm{O})(\mathrm{L}_{8}-\mathrm{H})_{6}\right]\) \(2\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{O}\cdot12\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) (36) was synthesized by treating an enantiopure \((\mathrm{D}/\mathrm{L})\) tartaric acid-derived building block \(\mathbf{L}_{8}\) with \(\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}\) in \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}/\mathrm{MeOH}\) under solvothermal conditions (Figure 14). In the resulting crystal structure, three zinc ions are held together with six carboxylate groups and a bridging oxo oxygen to form a \(\mathrm{Zn}_{3}(\mu_{3}-\mathrm{O})(\mathrm{carboxylate})_{6}\) secondary building unit (SBU), and the trinuclear unit can be further linked by coordinating Zn ions with some pyridyl groups to generate 2D layers with a hexagonal figure. Along the \(c\) axis, there are large \((\sim\!13.4\,\mathring{\mathrm{A}})\) chiral one-dimensional channels in this porous framework, the void volume of which is estimated to be
\(\sim\!47\%\) . Besides an integral part of the structure, the remaining pyridine molecules are exposed in the channels without any interactions with the framework, which endows the material with unique opportunities in catalysis (Figure 14).
Compound 36 was tested as a heterogeneous catalyst and revealed good enantioselective catalytic activity in the transesterification between an aromatic ester A and ethanol to produce ethyl acetate in \(77\%\) yield after reacting in carbon tetrachloride at \(27^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for 55h (Scheme 2). Transesterification of A with bulkier alcohols such as 2-butanol, neopentanol and 3,3,3-triphenyl-1-propanol occurred with a much slower or even negligible rate under otherwise identical reaction conditions, suggested the size-selective catalysis mainly occurs in the channels. The reaction of A with a large excess of racemic 1-phenyl-2-propanol in the presence of D-36 or the enantiomorphic L-36 produced the corresponding esters with \(\sim8\%\) enantiomeric excess in favor of \(S\) or \(R\) enantiomer, respectively. Although modest, it is the first, which breaks the deadlock that the modular porous materials mediated asymmetric induction is impossible and accelerates the development of MOF-based asymmetric catalysts.
In 2008, Rosseinsky and coworkers introduced Brøsted acidic sites into a chiral microporous MOF by post-synthesis for heterogeneous asymmetric catalysis of methanolysis of cis-2,3-epoxybutane.[85] Treatment of the amino acid-based open frameworks \([\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{L-aspartate})\mathrm{bpy}_{0.5}]\) and \(\left[\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{L-aspartate})(\mathrm{bpe})_{0.5}\right]\) (guests) with HCl in \(\mathrm{Et}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) afforded the protonated \([\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{L-aspartate})\mathrm{bpy}_{0.5}(\mathrm{HCl})_{0.9}(\mathrm{MeOH})_{0.5}]\) (37) and \(\left[\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{L-aspartate})(\mathrm{bpe})_{0.5}(\mathrm{HCl})\right.\) \(\left.\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\right)\right]\) (38), respectively (Figure 15). Infrared spectroscopic studies identified the sites of protonation located on the carboxylate groups of the aspartate ligands, giving rise to the formation of COOH groups as Brønsted-acidic sites in the solids. The two protonated products have gave similar PXRD patterns to their unprotonated forms, respectively, indicating framework connectivities were retained during the protonation process.
Both protonated solids were active as heterogeneous asymmetric catalyst in the methanolysis of ac-propylene oxide. The Ni-MOF 37 was less active owing to its nonporous nature, while the Cu-MOF 38 was porous and was able to uptake both the reactant molecules of methanol and propylene oxide, thereby exhibiting a significant improved reactivity in the methanolysis of propylene oxide (Scheme 3). Almost no conversion could be seen when bulkier (2,3-epoxypropyl)-benzene participated in the process, thus demonstrating the catalysis occurred mainly within the pores not just on the surface. Catalysis was proved to be heterogeneous as the filtered supernatant was inactive, also precluding possible catalysis by leached HCl. The Cu-framework structure remains intact after catalysis. Despite its homochiral framework structure, however, the chiral induction ability of the Cu-MOF 38 was modest in the methanolysis of several examined epoxides. The methanolysis of the meso epoxide, cis-2,3-epoxybutane afforded 3-methoxybutan-2-ol with a modest
\(17\ \%\) ee, and the methanolysis of racemic propylene oxide and trans-2,3-epoxybutane, both products were racemic.
In 2008, Tanaka and coworkers reported a chiral porous MOF built from the enantiopure ligand \(C_{2}\) -symmetric \(^{2,2^{\prime}}\) -dihydroxy\(1,1^{\prime}\) -binaphthalene- \(\cdot5,5^{\prime}\) -dicarboxylic acid \(\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{L}_{9}\right)\) and \({\mathrm{Cu}}(\mathrm{II})\) ion.[86] Slow diffusion of N,N-dimethylaniline into aqueous MeOH solution containing enantiopure ligand and copper nitrate gave rise to blue crystals of \([\mathrm{Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{L}_{9})_{2}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{2}]\cdot\mathrm{MeOH}\cdot2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) (39) (Figure 16). This compound has a 2D square grid coordination network with the paddle-wheel dinuclear copper carboxylate clusters as nodes. The grids stuck to form layers along the \(b\) -axis with an interlayer \(\mathrm{Cu}\) . . .Cu distance of \(15.6\,\mathrm{\AA}\) . Removal of the guest MeOH and \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) in vacuo led to an amorphous solid, which could be readily recovered to original crystalline phase upon exposing it to vapors of MeOH and water. The evacuated 39 was tested for catalysis of asymmetric ring opening reactions of epoxides with aromatic amines. After reacting at \(25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(48\mathrm{h}\) in toluene, optically active \(\beta\) -amino alcohol with a maximum \(54\%\) yield and \(45\%\) ee value was obtained (Scheme 4). The catalyst could be recovered by simple filtration to reuse in the next cycles without significant loss of reactivity and enantioselectivity. Interestingly, both the reactivity and enantioselectivity could be well improved when the reaction was carried out under solvent-free conditions. However, Control experiments showed that under the test conditions BINOL alone cannot act as an effective catalyst for the ring opening reaction.
In 2009, Wu and Wang et al. studied the catalytic activity of a homochiral MOF \([\mathrm{Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{L}_{10})_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}]\cdot\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) (40) in the Biginelli reaction of benzaldehyde, urea and ethyl acetoacetate and addition of Grignard reagent to ketones.[87] The compound 40 was synthesized by the reaction of the serine derivate ligand (S)-3- hydroxy-2-(pyridin-4-ylmethylamino)propanoic acid \(\left(\mathrm{L}_{10}\right)\) and \(\mathrm{CuCl}_{2}\) in \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}/\mathrm{MeOH}\) under solvothermal conditions. Each ligand chelates one copper atom through hydroxyl, carboxyl and amino groups and also bridges a neighboring copper atom via the pyridyl group to extend into 1D polymeric chains running along two different orientations. The chains are further coupled by one of the two chlorides to form a thick bilayer framework
structure, all of which stacked together via supramolecular interactions to form a 3D porous framework with 1D chiral channels \((5.1\,\times\,2.9\,\mathrm{~\AA~})\) occupied by water molecules. PXRP study showed the framework was maintained after removal of guest molecules.
The apohost 40 was examined for catalysis of the Biginelli reaction of benzaldehyde, urea and ethyl acetoacetate to give dihydropyrimidinone. The product was isolated in a \(90\%\) yield without any enantioselectivity. However, 40 could catalyze the addition of cyclohexylmagnesium chloride to \((E)\) -4-phenylbut-3-en-2-one to afford the 1,2-addition
product with \(97\%\) conversion and \(55\ \%\) ee, significantly different from the typical 1,4-addition promoted by copper compounds. The supernatant from the solid was inactive for the reaction, confirming the heterogeneous behavior of the catalyst system. A control experiment showed the free ligand can also promote the addition of cyclohexylmagnesium chloride to \((E)\) - 4-phenylbut-3-en-2-one but with lower conversion \((84\mathrm{~\textperthousand~}\) and ee \((51\%)\) . Therefore, the heterogeneous transformation is a typically organic functionality promoted process. The improved enantioselectivity may be due to homochiral amino groups in the solid 40 instead of the achiral center in \(\mathbf{L}_{10}\) ligand. Due to small pore sizes of the catalyst, the catalytic process was believed to take place on the catalyst surface rather than in the channels.
In 2009, Kim and coworkers reported an elegant study on the use of chiral catalytic MOFs generated from a preassembled achiral framework by post-synthesis for heterogeneous catalysis of the asymmetric aldol reactions.[64] In light of the robust structure, large pores, open metal coordination sites and insolubility, a chromium based framework MIL-101 was chosen as a parent framework for post-synthetic modification. The new chiral MOFs \([\mathrm{Cr}_{3}\mathrm{O}(\mathrm{L}_{11})_{1.8}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{0.25}(\mathrm{bdc})_{3}]\cdot0.15(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{bdc})\cdot\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) (41) and \([\mathrm{Cr}_{3}\mathrm{O}(\mathrm{L}_{12})_{1.75}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{0.25}\) \(\mathrm{F}(\mathrm{bdc})_{3}]\cdot0.15(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{bdc})\cdot\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) (42) were obtained by treating MIL-101 with chiral proline-derived \(\mathbf{L}_{11}\) ((S)-N-(pyridin-3-yl)-pyrrolidine-2-carboxamide) or \(\mathbf{L}_{12}\) ((S)- \(N\) -(pyridin-4-yl)-pyrrolidine-2- carboxamide) in a 1:3 ratio in refluxing chloroform for 1d, with their formulations supported by microanalysis and IR spectra. Both of the frameworks exhibited similar PXRD patterns to MIL-101, indicating comparable structures with the parent framework. Meanwhile, both chiral
MOFs were highly porous with BET surfaces \(1420~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}\) for 41 and \(1375~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}\) for 42 (Figure 17).
Asymmetric aldol reactions between various aromatic aldehydes and ketones could be efficiently catalyzed by these two heterogeneous catalysts in excellent yields \((60-90\%)\) and with good enantioselectivity for \(R\)-isomers \((ee\ 55-80\%)\) (Figure 17). For example, 41 could efficiently catalyze the aldol reaction between \(p\)-nitrobenzaldehyde and acetone at room temp in the absence of any added solvents with a decent yield \((66\%)\) and a fairly high enantioselectivity \((69\%~ee)\), while a control experiment with unmodified MIL-101 showed only \(10\%\) conversion and no ee at all. The same reaction when performed under homogeneous conditions with \(\mathbf{L}_{11}\) acting as a catalyst resulted in much lower ee \((29\%~ee)\). The solid catalysts always produced the aldol products with much higher enantiomeric excess than the corresponding homogeneous catalysts. The higher enantioselectivity may originate from the restricted movement of the substrates in the confined microporous systems in combination with multiple chiral inductions. Although both of them showed comparable catalytic efficiency, the former imposed better enantioselectivity in each case. This difference may, at least partly, be due to a bent ligand \(\mathbf{L}_{11}\) in 41 which may impose additional steric hindrance to the approaching aldehyde from \(\beta\)-face of the reaction site than 42 with a straight ligand \(\mathbf{L}_{12}\), thereby leading to higher enantioselectivity.
What's more, both of the catalysts can be reused for several cycles without much deterioration of activity both in yield and enantioselectivity. Although the large window size of the catalysts endows them with remarkable catalytic activities even towards large substituent reactants such as tert-butylcyclohexanone and para-nitrobenzaldehyde, the aldol reactions indeed occur mostly in the cavities of the catalysts rather than on the surface demonstrated by a size selectivity study. While the reaction between bulky aldehyde and para-nitrobenzaldehyde
in the presence of \(\mathbf{L}_{11}\) was completed within \(36~\mathrm{h}\), only \(5\%\) of the aldol product was observed in the presence of 41 after the same period of time. The solid catalyst can be recycled at least up to three times without obvious loss in reactivity and enantioselectivity.
## 4.2. Metal Catalysis
Lin and coworkers have designed a series of BINOL based homochiral bridging legends toward synthesizing homochiral porous MOFs for asymmetric catalysis. In 2001, Lin and coworkers prepared a family of homochiral MOFs \(\left[\mathrm{Ln}\left(\mathrm{L}_{13}-\mathrm{H}_{2}\right)\left(\mathrm{L}_{13}-\mathrm{H}_{3}\right)\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\right)_{4}\right]\cdot\mathrm{xH}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) \(\mathrm{Ln}=\mathrm{La}\), Ce, Pr, Nd, Sm, Gd, Tb, \(\mathbf{x}=\) 9-14, 43a–g) based on lanthanide phosphonate and examined their activities as Lewis acid catalysts by taking advantage of the lanthanide metal connecting points.[88] These 2D lamellar MOFs have asymmetric channels with largest dimensions of \(12\,\mathrm{\AA}\), and exhibit reversible dehydration and framework stability. The Sm compound 43e was an active chiral Lewis acid catalyst for several organic transformations, including cyanosilylation of aldehydes, ring opening of meso-carboxylic anhydride, and Diels-Alder reactions, but negligible ee values were observed for all these reactions. For the cyanosilylation and ring-opening reactions, catalysis was confirmed to be heterogeneous and the recovered Sm catalyst was reported to be reusable without loss of activity. This result illustrates the challenge in designing highly enantioselective MOF catalysts via remote influence by chiral environments of the open channels.
To enhance the enantioselectivity, Lin and coworkers reported the creation of a catalytically active chiral porous MOF by using chiral bridging ligands containing orthogonal functional groups.[89] The primary functional groups can be linked by metal-connecting units to form extended networks, whereas the orthogonal secondary functional groups can be used to generate the catalytic sites. Treatment of axially chiral bridging ligand \(\mathbf{L}_{14}\) (R)-6,6'-dichloro-2,2'-dihydroxy-1,1'-binaphthyl-4,4'-bipyridine with \(\mathrm{CdCl}_{2}\) gives colorless crystals of \(\left[\mathrm{Cd}_{3}\mathrm{Cl}_{6}(\mathrm{L}_{14})_{3}\right]\cdot4\mathrm{DMF}\cdot6\mathrm{MeOH}\cdot3\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) (44) in high yield by slow diffusion of diethyl ether into a mixture of ligand and \(\mathrm{CdCl}_{2}\) in MeOH/DMF (Figure 18). Single crystal X-ray analysis reveals that the octahedrally coordinated cadmium (II) ions are doubly bridged by two chlorides thus resulting in 1D zigzag chain of
\(\left[\mathrm{Cd}(\mu-\mathrm{Cl})_{2}\right]_{\mathrm{n}}\), which can be further linked as secondary building units through the linkage with the pyridyl groups of the ligands to form a non-interpenetrating 3D network with large chiral channels about \(1.6\times1.8~\mathrm{nm}\) and a specific surface area of \(601~\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}\). While two-thirds hydroxyl groups are shielded, the remaining third are exposed into the channels, thus facilitating the post-synthesis with \(\mathrm{Ti}(\mathrm{O}^{\mathrm{i}}\mathrm{Pr})_{4}\) to afford a chiral catalytic species.
After post synthesized with \(\mathrm{Ti}(\mathrm{O}^{\mathrm{i}}\mathrm{Pr})_{4}\), a Lewis acidic titanium complex 44·Ti was obtained, which can enantioselectively catalyze the addition of \(\mathrm{ZnEt}_{2}\) to aldehydes with enantiomeric excesses over \(90\%\) comparable to homogeneous analogue (Scheme 5). The
supernatant solution of the reaction medium containing the immobilized catalyst did not show any activity, thus suggesting the catalyst was truly heterogeneous. The 44·Ti catalyzed \( \mathrm{ZnEt}_{2} \) addition is a "in-channel" heterogeneous reaction, demonstrated by a series of contrast tests performed by dendritic aldehydes with varying sizes from \( 0.8\mathrm{nm} \) to \( 2\mathrm{nm} \). As a result, larger aldehydes led to lower conversions whereas the efficiency for homogeneously catalyzed \( \mathrm{ZnEt}_{2} \) additions was not affected by the dendron size. Besides size selectivity, the high catalytic activity even with lower catalyst loading of 44·Ti is obvious, in contrast to some analogous homogeneous catalysts.
On the basis of the above achievements, the Lin group further extended the strategy to the preparation of similar chiral porous MOFs.[90] Still the same ligand \( \mathbf{L}_{14} \), the same diffusion condition with only \( \mathrm{CdCl}_{2} \) replaced by \( \mathrm{Cd}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2} \) or \( \mathrm{Cd}(\mathrm{ClO}_{4})_{2} \) eventually led to two new homochiral metal-organic porous networks \( [\mathrm{Cd}_{3}(\mathbf{L}_{14})_{4}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{6}]\cdot7\mathrm{MeOH}\cdot5\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O} \) (45) and \( [\mathrm{Cd}(\mathbf{L}_{14})_{2}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{2}]\cdot[\mathrm{ClO}_{4}]_{2}\cdot\mathrm{DMF}\cdot4\mathrm{MeOH}\cdot3\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O} \) (46). Both of the frameworks are highly interpenetrated 3D networks, specifically, in 45 the 2D grids from \( \mathbf{L}_{14} \) and \( \mathrm{Cd}^{11} \) and the derived 1D zigzag chains are connected by bridging nitrate groups to afford a 3D framework structure while in 46 the two-fold interpenetrating network is constructed uniquely by 2D rhombic grids. Despite the highly structural interpenetration, there are still large interconnected channels with dimensions of approximately \( 4.9\times13.1\mathrm{~\AA~} \) along the \( a \) and \( b \) axes and \( 13.5\times13.5\mathrm{~\AA~} \) along the \( c \) axis in 45 and \( 12\times15\mathrm{~\AA~} \) in 46 respectively. After treating 45 with excess \( \mathrm{Ti}(\mathrm{O}^{i}\mathrm{Pr})_{4} \) in toluene, 44·Ti can be obtained as an active heterogeneous catalyst for the addition of diethylzinc to a range of aromatic aldehydes to afford chiral secondary alcohols with complete conversion and \( 45{-}90\% \) ee values. The conversions and ee values are only slightly affected by catalyst loadings. No secondary alcohol product was obtained when 1-naphthaldehyde was treated with diethylzinc in the presence of the supernatant from a mixture of 45 and \( \mathrm{Ti}(\mathrm{O}^{i}\mathrm{Pr})_{4} \) under otherwise identical conditions, indicative of the heterogeneous nature of this catalyst system
\[
\mathsf{Ar}\xrightarrow{\mathsf{O}}\mathsf{C}\xrightarrow{\mathsf{H}}\mathsf{ZnEt}_2\xrightarrow{\mathsf{44\cdot Ti}}\mathsf{Ar}\xrightarrow{\mathsf{C}}\mathsf{C}
\]
Scheme 5. Asymmetric diethylzinc addition to aromatic aldehydes catalyzed by 44·Ti.
In sharp contrast with 45, a combination of 46 and \( \mathrm{Ti}(\mathrm{O}^{i}\mathrm{Pr})_{4} \) under identical conditions had no asymmetric catalytic activity towards this reaction at all, even though there are similar permanent porosity and chiral environment as in 45, probably as the result of the steric hindrance around the chiral dihydroxy groups that prevents the formation of Ti-binolate catalyst. A careful examination of the structure of 46 reveals that the pyridyl and naphthyl rings from mutually perpendicular, interpenetrating 2D grids have strong \( \pi\cdot\cdot\pi \) interactions, leading to the dihydroxy groups of the ligands very close to the \( \{\mathrm{Cd}(\mathrm{py})_{2}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{2}\} \) hinges. The lack of catalytic activity with the \( 46/\mathrm{Ti}(\mathrm{O}^{i}\mathrm{Pr})_{4} \) system is thus ascribed to the steric congestion around the chiral dihydroxy groups, which prevent the substitution of two isopropoxide groups by binol hydroxyl groups. Completely different catalytic activities arising from nuances in framework structures demonstrated by this finding imply the structure-dependent activity for heterogeneous catalysts and will promise the diversity of MOF-based asymmetric catalysis influenced by minor change with components.
Incorporation of asymmetric catalysts directly into the porous MOF was also studied by Nguyen, Hupp and coworkers. In 2006, Nguyen and Hupp et al. reported a microporous MOF compound featuring chiral salen-Mn struts for heterogeneous catalysis of asymmetric olefin epoxidation.[91] The crystalline solid \( \mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{bpd}c)_{2}\mathrm{L}_{15}\cdot10\mathrm{DMF}\cdot8\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O} \) (47) was obtained by treating of functionalized salen ligand \( \mathrm{L}_{15}\cdot(R,R)\cdot(-1,2 \cdot \) -cyclohexanediamino- \( N,N^{\prime} \) -bis(3-tert-butyl -5-(4-pyridyl)salicylidene) \( \mathrm{Mn}^{1-} \) \( ^{11}\mathrm{Cl} \) with corresponding zinc salt and biphenyldicarboxylic acid \( (\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{bpd}c) \) in DMF under solvothermal conditions (Figure 19). It is a twofold interpenetrating 3D network for 47 with all \( \mathrm{Mn}^{11} \) sites accessible to the channels, where distorted-rectangular and rhombic channels along the \( a \) and \( c \) directions possess dimensions of \( 6.2\times6.2\mathrm{~\AA~} \) and \( 6.2\times15.7\mathrm{~\AA~} \). PXRD measurements showed that the evacuated sample retains crystallinity. However, diffraction peaks are shifted, implying structural distortions. Notably, resolution by DMF reverses the shifts. TGA measurements with resolvated material confirmed that porosity is retained.
To examine the catalytic activity towards asymmetric olefin epoxidation, 47 catalyzed the epoxidation of 2,2-dimethyl-2H-chromene using 2-(tert-butylsulfonyl)iodosylbenzene as oxidant was carried out, resulting in the desired product in 71% yield
and \(82\%\) ee (Scheme 6). Unlike the homogeneous catalyst, which is highly effective but easily inactivated, the immobilized catalyst 47 displayed close to constant activity with higher total turnover numbers. Beyond catalytic activity, the recyclability of 47 was remarkable, with almost no loss of enantioselectivity and only a slight activity decrease after three cycles. This excellent performance may be ascribed to the rigid structure of the catalytic complex, avoiding oxidation of the salen ligand by cross-reaction between two catalyst molecules. Inductively coupled plasma (ICP) analysis of the product solution showed that between 4 and \(7\%\) of the manganese was lost per cycle – either as molecular species or as particles too small to be removed by filtration. To check whether catalysis occurs predominantly within the framework channel or instead on the external surface, competitive size selectivity studies were performed with a large porphyrinic substrate (too large to enter the channels) and a small substrate, ethyl 4-vinyl benzoate in a 1:1 molar ratio. Initially, the ratio of small to large substrate reactivity was 2, but at \(45\%\) conversion the reactivity ratio had increased to 18. Control experiments with the homogeneous catalyst gave a time independent 1:1 reactivity ratio. Evidently, at the beginning a significant fraction of the catalysis occurs on the MOF surface, but at later stages catalysis occurs chiefly within the MOF channels.
## 5. Enantioselective Separation
Beyond asymmetric catalysis, homochiral MOFs have also shown potential in enantioselective separation of racemic organic molecules. Since 1992, Mallouk et al. pioneered the optical resolution of organic molecules through intercalation by a layered phosphate with an optically active building block,[92] it has been a continuing challenge and ambitious task for
chemists to achieve enantioseparation of racemic organic molecules by recyclable metal-organic assemblies or sieves. Specially, in 2000, Kim and coworkers employed the chiral building block (optical active or homochiral) to construct a chiral 2D layered metal coordination polymer to resolve racemic \(\mathrm{[Ru-(2,2^{\prime}-bipy)_{3}]}\) \(\mathrm{Cl}_{3}\) (bipy = bipyridine) with a \(66\%\) enantiomeric excess (ee) in favor of the \(\Delta\) form.[19] However, examples of enantioselective sorption and/or enantioseparation are still rather rare in the literature. Resolution of enantiomers through selective crystallization of diastereomeric inclusion compounds mediated by porous MOFs remains a challenging task.
## 5.1. Adsorption Separation
In 2001, Xiong and coworkers created the precedent of enantioselective sorption and separation capable of resolving racemic mixtures of small organic molecules based on a homochiral 3D zeolite-like MOF (Figure 20).[93] The compound \(\mathrm{[Cd(QA)_{2}]}\) 48 was obtained by treating enantiopure chiral bridging ligand quitenine (6'-methoxyl-(8S,9R)-cinchonen-9-ol-3-carboxylic acid (HQA)) with \(\mathrm{Cd(OH)_{2}}\)-under solvothermal conditions. The crystal structure shows that \(\mathrm{Cd}^{2+}\) ion is in a distorted octahedral geometry and coordinated by two nitrogen atoms and four oxygen atoms of two bidentate carboxylate groups, thus each \(\mathrm{Cd}^{2+}\) ion is coordinated by four ligands that bridge to four other symmetry-related Cd ions, generating a 3D diamond-like net. The absence of interpenetration in 48 results in a lattice with maximum space available for the guest. The chirality of the network further suggests that the intraframework voids may provide a chiral environment for guest molecules.
Under solvothermal conditions, treating 48 and racemic 2-butanol afforded colorless crystalline sample 48-(S)-2-butanol. A single-crystal X-ray diffraction study performed on it clearly reveals that (S)-2-butanol was wrapped in the chiral cavities of the network. The included small alcohols can be completely removed upon heating the samples at appropriate temperature.
The ee value of the desorbed (S)-2-butanol was estimated to be approximately \(98.2\%\) by comparing its optical rotation with pure (S)-2-butanol standard. Similarly, 48·(S)-2-methyl-1-butanol can also be obtained from racemic 2-methyl-1-butanol. The solid-state structure shows that it is isostructural with 48·(S)-2-butanol with the guest alcohols in the chiral cavities, and that the 2-methyl-1-butanol included is the S enantiomer. The optical rotation of the desorbed 2-methyl-1-butanol is identical to the standard (S)-2-methyl-1-butanol with an ee value of about \(8.4\%\).
Creating homochiral MOFs from achiral building blocks capable of resolving racemic molecules is always a great challenge. In 2006, Xiong and coworkers described an example of a spontaneously resolved complex derived from achiral components used as sorbents for chiral species.[94] The homochiral MOF \(\left[\mathrm{Cu}\left(\mathrm{PPh}_{3}\right)\left(N,N^{\prime}\right.\right.\) -(2-pyridyl-(4-pyridylmethyl)-amine) \()_{1.5}]\cdot\mathrm{ClO}_{4}\) (49), crystallizing in the trigonal \(R_{3}\) space group, was harvested under solvothermal conditions from achiral \(N,N^{\prime}\) -(2-pyridyl-(4-pyridyl methyl)-amine) and \(\left[\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{MeCN})_{2}(\mathrm{PPh}_{3})_{2}\right]\mathrm{ClO}_{4}\). It's a chiral 2D metal-organic coordination polymer with triangular cavities, definitely confirmed by X-ray single crystal diffraction. Crystals of 49 could be mechanically separated into groups according to each crystal identical CD spectra in solid state. Solvothermal treatment of powder 49 with a solution of racemic 2-butanol at \(60\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for 1 day afforded colorless crystalline sample of 49·(S)-2-butanol. Single-crystal X-ray analysis show there are 2-butanol molecules residing in a triangular center in the crystal structure. Each two 2D layers intercalate disordered 2-butanol molecules. The (S)-configuration of included 2-butanol was confirmed by comparing with that of the standard sample. The resolution ee value of racemic 2-butanol is estimated to be approximately \(30\%\).
In 2004, Rosseinsky and coworkers reported several homochiral MOFs displaying enantioselective sorption that is strongly dependent on guest size.[95] \(\left[\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{btc})_{2}(\mathrm{py})_{6}(1,2\mathrm{-pd})_{3}\right]\cdot11(1,2\mathrm{-pd})\cdot8\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) (50) and \(\left[\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{btc})_{2}(3\mathrm{-pic})_{6}(1,2\mathrm{-pd})_{3}\right]\cdot9(1,2\mathrm{-pd})\cdot11\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) (51) were prepared by slow diffusion of pyridine or picoline into a solution of \(\mathrm{H}_{3}(\mathrm{btc})\) and nickel nitrates in the presence of 1,2-propanediol as a co-ligand (Figure 21). The \(\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}\) ion is
six-coordinated with the trans diaxial positions occupied by two tridentate btc ligands, the two cis equatorial positions occupied by two pyridine or picoline and the other two adjacent equatorial positions fixed by a bidentate 1,2-propanediol, which controls the helix chirality within the interpenetrating (10,3)-a nets. PXRD and gas sorption data show that 50 collapses upon removal of the guests in the pore system and is thus not microporous. Interestingly, treating this amorphous solid with pyridine leads to a nonporous layered structure in which the btc and \(\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}\) centers form a 2D (6,3) net, while treating it with 3-picoline affords solely the 2D chiral interpenetrating (10,3)-a structure. In strong contrast to 50, the crystallinity of 51 remains intact after complete removal of guest molecules, as shown by PXRD. The permanent porosity of 51 is confirmed by its \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) adsorption isotherm, from which a surface area of 930(15) \(\mathrm{m}^{2}\,\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) is obtained. The measured micropore volume of 0.63(1) \(\mathrm{ml}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) corresponds well to the porosity estimate of \(47\%\) derived from the extraframework volume in the structure and the estimated density of \(0.8\;\mathrm{ml}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\).
The large chiral pores and channels of 51 give a selectivity for chiral guest molecules that depends strongly on their size. The chiral guest species chosen for the testing of enantioselectivity are ethyl-3-hydroxybutyrate, the terpenes menthone, fenchone and the aromatic diol binaphthol. The racemic samples are readily taken up by the evacuated 51 from the liquid or vapor phase, and are removed from the resolved frameworks by washing with dichloromethane. While chiral-GC reveals no enantioselection for the former three desorbed chiral species, the solid binaphthol guest is sorbed by apohost 51 with an enantiomeric excess of \(8.3\%\) (as determined by HPLC analysis) in competition uptake experiments from a racemic mixture in dichloromethane solution (Figure 21). The total amount of binaphthol sorbed by the desolvated material was determined as \(7.3\%\) uptake by mass (from the guests washed out from the binaphthol-loaded material). Obviously, ethyl 3-hydroxybutyrate \((6\times3\mathrm{~\AA})\), menthone \((8\times5\mathrm{~\AA})\) and fenchone \((5\times4\mathrm{~\AA})\) are too small compared to the channel dimensions to display appreciable enantioselection. The largest chiral guest tested is binaphthol which, although its perpendicular dimensions at \(9\times9\mathrm{~\AA~}\) are smaller than both the estimated and minimum measured channel diameter, is sorbed by apohost 51 with a very modest ee value. Thus, 51 is capable of enantioselective sorption to an extent dependent on guest size. A close match to the channel dimensions is required to bring the chiral guests sufficiently close to the helical internal surface of porous material to allow for discrimination between the enantiomers.
Based on the above achievements, in 2006, Rosseinsky et al. reported nanoporous MOFs based on amino acid backbone for racemic alcohol separation.[96] The open-framework \(\left[\mathrm{Ni}_{2}(\mathrm{L}-\mathrm{asp})_{2}(\mathrm{bipy})\right]\cdot\mathrm{xCH}_{3}\mathrm{OH}\cdot\mathrm{yH}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) (52) was synthesized by solvothermal reaction of \(\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{asp})\cdot3\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) with \(4,4^{\prime}\)-bipyridine (bipy) in a water and methanol mixture at \(150\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) (Figure 22). Chiral GC confirms that the enantiopurity of the starting amino acid is delivered to the product by this route when either D- or L-aspartate is used as the starting material. In 52, the neutral chiral \(\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{L}-\mathrm{asp})\) layers are connected by \(4,4^{\prime}\)-bipyridine linkers to afford a pillared structure with about \(3.8\times4.7\,\mathrm{\AA}\) 1D channels defined by the length of the \(4,4^{\prime}\)-bipyridine ligand and the separation between the nickel centers in the \(\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{L}-\mathrm{asp})\) layer. The
pillared structure has \(\sim23.1\%\) solvent-accessible volume. In addition, the replacement of \(4,4^{\prime}\)-bipyridine with related but longer bridging nitrogen-donor ligands generates analogous materials with larger channels. PXRD study indicates that 52 could retain its open-framework structure upon loss of the methanol and water guest molecules from the channels. The permanent porosity of 52 is confirmed by \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) adsorption isotherm, from which a BET surface area of \(247(3)\;\mathrm{m}^{2}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) is obtained.
In view of the homochiral nature of the biologically derived internal surface arising from the enantiopure aspartate units, the enantioselective sorption property of 52 was probed by using a library of small chiral molecules with closely related functionalities. The levels of enantioselection for the sorption of small alcohols are significant, but it is the differentiation between diols with similar chain lengths but differing separations between the hydroxyl groups which is particularly striking. For a fixed chain length of four carbon atoms, 1,2-butanediol and 2,3-butanediol display considerably reduced enantioselection with respect to 1,3-butanediol. The favorable nature of the 1,3-disposition of the diol units
is further confirmed by the high ee value of 2,4-pentanediol, in comparison with those of 1,2-pentanediol and 2,5-hexanediol. The highest sorption ee value observed within the library of diol guests studied was \(53.7\%\) for 2-methyl-2,4-pentanediol (Figure 22).
The characteristics of the interactions between a diol guest and the internal surface of a porous material composed of Ni(asp) layers and \(4,4^{\prime}\)-bipyridine pillars are revealed by the crystal structure of \([\mathrm{Ni}_{2}(\mathrm{D},\mathrm{L}-\mathrm{asp})_{2}(\mathrm{bipy})]\cdot1,2\)-propanediol (53), which has a similar pillared structure to 52. Structural analysis reveals that the alcohol guest is located in a pocket defined by two neighboring \(4,4^{\prime}\)-bipyridine molecules and adopts an extended conformation, with the diol groups hydrogen bonded to the oxygen atoms of carboxylate groups in two Ni(asp) layers. Computational study further indicates the differentiation of substrates in 53 on the basis of multiple supramolecular interactions within a compositionally complex pocket, which is
reminiscent of the selective function of enzymes. Therefore, besides good match of size and shape between the host and the desired guest, the channel surface chemistry is also of significant importance in chiral recognition and separation.
In 2008, Cui and coworkers presented that large arrays of cyclic clusters with enzyme-like chiral cavities are promising materials for applications in separating chemical species.[97,98] Heating a mixture of the semiflexible pyridyl-functionalized salen-based ligand \(\mathrm{L}_{16}\) with \(\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}\cdot6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) in a mixed solvents of DMF- \(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{CN}\) and DMF-THF led to crystalline products \([\mathrm{Zn}_{4}(\mathrm{L}_{16})_{4}]\cdot4\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{CN}\) (54) and \([\mathrm{Zn}_{6}(\mathrm{L}_{16})_{6}]\cdot6\mathrm{THF}\) (55), respectively (Figure 23-24). Both compounds feature an unsaturated zinc centre and two semi-flexible arms with terminal pyridyl groups. Four or six of these complexes were then coordinated to form a tetrameric or hexameric cyclic cluster with a chiral cavity at the core with \(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{CN}\) or THF as template molecules. The four or six pairs of tert-butyl groups of the \(\{\mathrm{ZnL}_{16}\}\) units are organized such that they partly cover two sides of the cyclic cluster along generating four or six hydrophobic pockets. Highly directional noncovalent interactions including intermolecular \(\pi...\pi\) and \(\mathrm{CH}_{\cdot\cdot\cdot\pi}\) interactions have steered the packing of the tetrameric cycles into a neutral, porous 3D structure with equilateral-triangle-shaped zigzag channels of \(7.0\mathrm{~\AA~}\), and the hexameric cycles into a porous 3D nanotubular architecture with an opening size of \(1.4\times1.1\;\mathrm{nm}\). Single-crystal XRD and PRXD showed that both 54 and 55 could undergo reversible single-crystal to single-crystal structural transformations in response to guest removal and uptake. Powder XRD experiments indicate that the framework and crystallinity of the two samples remain intact upon complete removal of guest molecules. The permanent porosity of 55 was confirmed by its \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) adsorption isotherm, from which a Langmuir surface area of \(504\;\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}\) and a pore volume of \(0.48\;\mathrm{mL}/\mathrm{g}\) were obtained.
This resolution of 2-butanol by 54 was achieved by soaking the evacuated sample in neat racemic alcohols in a sealed vial
at \(40^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for 2-3 days. This inclusion of 2-butanol by the apohost formulated as \(54\cdot2\)-butanol is supported by microanalysis, TGA, and powder XRD. Chiral GC analysis of the desorbed 2-butanol from (R)-54 by distillation showed the ee value was \(99.8\%\) and the absolute \(R\) configuration was obtained by comparing its optical rotation with that of the standard sample (Figure 23). Similar enantioselective inclusion was observed for racemic 3-methyl-2-butanol, whereby 54 exhibited remarkable sorption toward the \(R\) enantiomer over the \(S\) enantiomer as well. The ee value of the desorbed 3-methyl-2-butanol was determined to be \(99.6\%\) by chiral GC analysis. The solid could then be retrieved and used again, although with lower selectivity \((91.6\%)\) for resolving racemic 3-methyl-2-butanol. However, the longer alcohols such as 2-pentanol can not be separated due to its small cavity.
Compound 55 can be used to resolve racemic alcohols in the same way as that for 54. In this case, the 2-pentanol can be included and separated. This inclusion of 2-pentanol is also supported by microanalysis, TGA, and PXRD. The solid-state structure clearly revealed that the 55·6(R)-2-pentanol adduct is isostructural to 55·6THF and (R)-2-pentanol has been included in the chiral channel. The cell volume of adduct 55 increases by \(2.6\%\) as THF molecules were replaced by pentanol molecules in the structure. Although the tubular skeleton is intact, the \(\mathrm{ZnL}_{16}\) units undergo rotational rearrangements upon including larger guest molecules. Six free alcohol molecules are trapped in six pockets of the macrocycle and form weak hydrophobic interactions through their methyl groups. More interestingly, the hydroxy groups of six alcohol molecules form strong hydrogen bonds leading to a chair-shaped conformation. Chiral GC analysis of the 2-pentanol desorbed from 55 showed that the ee value was \(99.5\%\), with the absolute \(R\) configuration (Figure 24). Similar enantioselective inclusion behaviors are observed for racemic 2-butanol and 3-methyl-2-butanol, whereby it exhibited remarkable sorption toward the \(R\) enantiomer over the \(S\) enantiomer as well, with ee values \(98.4\%\) and \(95.6\%\) respectively. The separator 55 could be used for at least five cycles without loss of enantioselectivity and crystallinity. Attempts to separate racemic 2-hexanol by using 55 failed. As adsorption materials, the enantioselective properties of 54 and 55 maybe haven't been studied more in-depth, but the preliminary research results have provided great inspiration for constructing enantioselective crystalline solids through non-covalent interactions.
