In 2001, Lin and coworkers reported the utility of ammonia treated samples of 43e in chiral separation.[88] Racemic trans-1,2-diaminocyclohexane was separated with \(R\)-43e at a substrate/host ratio of 1.4 and an enantioenrichment of \(13.6\%\) in \(S\),S-1,2-diaminocyclohexane in the beginning fractions and an enantio-enrichment of \(10.0\%\) in \(R\),R-1,2-diaminocyclohexane in the ending fractions.
In 2006, Fedin and Kim et al. investigated the separation and catalytic properties
of a homochiral porous MOF for enantioselective adsorption and oxidation of thioethers.[99] The porous material \(\left[\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{bdc})\right]\) (L-lac)(dmf)·(DMF) (56) was prepared by heating a mixture of \(\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}\), L-lactic acid and 1,4-benzenedicarboxylic acid \(\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{bdc}\right)\) in DMF at \(110\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for 2 days. The zinc ions and lactate ligands form 1D chiral chains along the \(a\)-axis. These chains are interlinked by benzenedicarboxylate spacers in the other two directions to give a 3D neutral porous MOF, and the pores of \(\sim5\,\mathrm{\AA}\) diameter are interconnected in three directions. The chiral centers of the L-lactate moieties are exposed within the channels. PXRD showed that some of the guest DMF molecules can be removed from the channels to afford a partially evacuated 56 with permanent porosity and retention of crystallinity.
The partially evacuated 56 was stirred in a \(\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}\) solution containing a racemic mixture of sulfoxides for \(16\,\mathrm{h}\), collected, and washed with methanol to extract adsorbed molecules. The obtained material showed an excellent sorption capability to the sulfoxides with smaller substituents. The ee values for the adsorbed guests were \(20\%\sim27\%\), with the \(S\) enantiomer in excess for both cases. However, sulfoxides with larger substituents could not be included in any significant amount. Such size- and enantioselective guest inclusion indicates that 56 has a robust structure with pores of uniform size and chiral environment. The porous materials could be readily regenerated by extracting the adsorbed guest molecules and reused in the next cycles of sorption experiments, without appreciable loss of its sorption capability. In addition, 56 can also mediate highly size- and chemoselective catalytic oxidation of thioethers to sulfoxides by urea hydroperoxide (UHP) or \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\), suggesting the reaction primarily occurs inside the micropores of the material. Although chemoselectivity toward the sulfoxides was good to
excellent, no asymmetric induction was observed in the catalytic sulfoxidations despite the homochiral nature of the solid catalyst.
Inspired by this above success, in 2007, Bryliakov and Fedin et al. developed a way of resolution of sulfoxides using 56 as the chiral stationary phase and a unique one-pot process of synthesis of enantiomerically pure sulfoxides based on the combined chromatographic and catalytic properties of 56 (Figure 25).[100] The \(33\ \mathrm{cm}\) high chiral column was prepared by loading a glass tube ( \(8\ \mathrm{mm}\) inner diameter) with a suspension of 56 in \(10\%\) solution of DMF in \(\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}\), and a mixture of the sulfide (PhSMe) and \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) was charged directly onto the top of the column. Each probe contained ca. \(0.15\ \mathrm{mmol}\) of the sulfoxide in \(0.2\ \mathrm{cm}^{3}\) of \(\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}\). In the experimental chromatogram, the sulfoxide, PhSOMe demonstrated a clear peak resolution that allowed the complete separation of enantiomers. Additional electron-withdrawing substituents (Br- and \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\)-) in the aromatic ring of the sulfoxides reduced both the sorption constants and ee's of sorption. Introduction of an electron-donating substituent (p-MePhSOMe and PhSOMe) resulted in higher sorption constants but lower stereoselectivity factor that, along with the slower internal diffusion of \(p\)-MePhSOMe, led to poorer enantioseparation. Steric effect was also examined by separating PhSOi-Pr. The enantioseparation was incomplete probably due to the higher diffusion obstacles for the bulkier \(i\)-Pr substituent at the sulfur atom.
The combined oxidation/separation experiments were performed using the same column as mentioned above. A mixture of the sulfide (PhSMe) and \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) was charged directly onto the top of the column. Using \(\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{CN}\) mixture as the eluent, \((R)\)- and \((S)\)-enantiomers of the sulfoxide (PhSOMe) could be collected separately in enantiopure form at the other
end of the column. The effect of oxidant amount on the product composition was also studied. The use of 5-fold excess of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) leads to slight overoxidation of PhSOMe. The yield of enantiopure \((ee=100\%)\) fraction of \((S)\)-PhSOMe was estimated. ca. \(35\%\). The use of 1.5-fold excess of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) leads to some sulfide unreacted (conversion \(91\%\)). In this case, both \((R)\)- and \((S)\)-sulfoxides could be isolated with relatively high yields because the sulfide comes out from the column before PhSOMe. This work is the first documented use of MOFs as chiral stationary phases for preparative liquid chromatography
## 5.2. Crystallization Separation
Crystallization, along with adsorption, is one of the most important methods for the recovery of pure enantiomers.[101] Although the optical resolution of organic substrates by lattice inclusion using a pure organic host has received significant attention,[102–104] the crystallization process involved in the synthesis of MOF materials has not in itself been explored as a tool for chiral separation. Note that synthesis of homochiral MOFs from racemic precursors by spontaneous resolution has also attracted considerable interest (see section 2).
In 2009, Justyniak and Lewiński et al. described the use of homochiral nanotubules of cinchoninate to resolve 2-methyltetrahydrofurane (Me-THF).[105] Treatment of aluminum complexes \(\left(\mathrm{AlMe}_{2}\mathrm{Cl}\right.\) or \(\left.\mathrm{AlMe}_{3}\right)\) with 2 equiv of cinchonine \(\left(\mathrm{L}_{17}\mathrm{-H}\right)\) afforded the bischelate complexes \(\left[(\mathrm{L}_{17})_{2}\mathrm{AlX}\right]\) \(\left(\mathrm{X}=\mathrm{Cl}\ 57\right.\), Me 58) (Figure 26). In both cases, \(\left[(\mathrm{L}_{17})_{2}\mathrm{AlX}\right]\) molecules are organized through noncovalent interactions into extended quasi-honeycomb networks with 1D helical tubular channel. The average inner diameter of the channels is \(\sim8\ \mathrm{\AA}\), and the free space is
filled by the solvent molecules. The helical organization of the bischelate complexes leads to the exposed electronegative atoms inside the channels, holding potential for discrimination between chiral guests by inclusion crystallization.
To test the resolution ability of this \(\left[\left(\mathbf{L}_{17}\right)_{2}\mathbf{AlX}\right]\) system, 58 was dissolved in \((\pm)\)-Me-THF and then single crystals crystallized from this solvent were collected. The crystal structure analysis revealed that the replacement of the THF solvent by Me-THF did not affect the supramolecular architecture of the resulting framework. Optical rotatory power measurements on the recovered Me-THF showed a small enantiomeric enrichment of \(\sim7\%\) ee (Figure 26). After removal of the guest, crystals of 58 could be recycled and utilized for next resolution. In addition, Complexes 57 and 58 have also been used as N-ditopic metalloligands to construct 1D helical MOF, which may improve their enantioselective recognition abilities.
Based on the earlier findings, in 2009, Cui and coworkers developed an efficient approach for highly enantioselective separation of small racemic alcohols by inclusion crystallization.[79] Solvothermal reaction of
Based on the earlier findings, in 2009, Cui and coworkers developed an efficient approach for highly enantioselective separation of small racemic alcohols by inclusion crystallization.[79] Solvothermal reaction of copper nitrates with chiral carboxylic acid-functionalized metal-losalen ligand \((R,R)\cdot\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{L}_{18}\) \((3\cdot[(E)\cdot]\{[(R,2)R]\cdot2\cdot\) -aminocyclohexyl-imino}-methyl]-4-hydroxybenzoic acid) in the presence of alcohols (MeOH, EtOH and \(i{\mathrm{-PrOH}}\) ) afforded lamellar MOFs, \([\mathrm{Cu}_{2}(\mathbf{L}_{18})_{2}]\cdot2\mathrm{X}\) (59·2X, \(X=\mathrm{MeOH}\) , EtOH and \(i{\mathrm{-PrOH}}\) ). Single crystal X-ray structural study revealed that tridentate Schiff base \(\mathbf{L}_{18}\) ligands link adjacent metal centers to form 1D polymeric chains using tridentate N2O donors and carboxylate groups, while interchain metal–oxygen and hydrogen-bonding interactions further link 1D polymeric chains to a porous lamellar solid. The host features a flexible helical framework for inclusion of different size of alcohol and chiral amphiphilic channel surfaces lined with amine hydrogen atoms and aliphatic groups that may be capable of multiple interactions with guest species.
This inclusion of 2-butanol by the host structure was achieved through crystallization of host (R)-59 with racemic alcohols. Chiral GC analysis of the desorbed 2-butanol from (R)-59 by distillation or washed with \(\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}\) showed the ee value was \(99.8\%\) (Figure 27). Similar enantioselective inclusion behaviors are observed for racemic 3-methyl-2-butanol and 2-pentanol, whereby compound (R)-59 exhibited remarkable inclusion toward the \(R\) enantiomer over the S enantiomer as well. The inclusion compounds can be formulated as 59·2(3-methyl-2-butanol) and 59·2(2-pentanol), respectively, based on elemental analysis and TGA. The ee values of the desorbed alcohols were 99.6 and \(99.5\%\) , respectively. The chiral nature of the included molecule is determined by the handedness of the host, as further evidenced by the fact that (S)-59 showed remarkable inclusion toward the S enantiomer over the \(R\) enantiomer as well \((99.6\%\) ee). However, 2-heptanol and 2-octanol could not produce inclusion crystals with 59 under otherwise identical
crystallization conditions, probably due to that the two alcohols are too large to be trapped in the frameworks. The \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{L}_{18}\) ligand can be easily recycled by using dilute HCl to dissociate the used powder and by extraction with chloroform.
The structural arrangement of the host/guest revealed by single-crystal X-ray analysis provides insight into the origin of the stereoselectivity of the system. The inclusion complex is isostructural to 59·2X. The guest (R)-2-butanol is located in the groove of the supramolecular helices and adopts an extended conformation, with the OH group hydrogen bonded to the coordinated amine of \(\mathbf{L}_{18}\) in the polymer. This enforces a chiral \(P2_{1}\) symmetric cavity in which inclusion of (R)-2-butanol evades steric interactions between the terminal alkyl group and the cyclohexane groups of \(\mathbf{L}_{18}\), which an included S enantiomer could not evade. Both the helical pitches of the intertwined supramolecular chains and the layer-to-layer separation increase for entrapping 2-butanol molecules. The stretch of intertwined helices is also accompanied by the self-adjustment variation of hydrogen bonds and \(\mathrm{Cu\cdot\cdot\cdotO}\) interactions within the infinite chains. Therefore, the potential barrier for expanding the helical pitch in 59 is small and can be readily overcome by the inclusion of C1 to C5 alcohols. Whereas the size of the included guest is mainly determined by the intrinsic pore shape and size of the host, the ability of 59 to tune helical pitches and interlayer separations according to guest molecules is limited and so the longer C6 and C7 alcohols were excluded. The differentiation of substrates on the basis of multiple weak interactions and cooperative interactions within a compositionally complex chiral groove is reminiscent of the selective function of enzymes. Overall, the above success arises from the flexible host framework coupled with an amphiphilic channel, leading
