to bioanalogous recognition behavior during interaction with guest species.
## 6. Outlook and Conclusion
While tremendous progress has been made in synthesis of MOFs with new structural topologies, exciting opportunities remain for functionalizing such materials to optimize their properties for specific applications.[106] In this review, we have summarized the latest developments of homochiral MOFs for applications in heterogeneous asymmetric catalysis and enantioselective separation.
The synthetic strategies that have been developed for homochiral MOFs are continually being improved. In the direct approach, particularly that based on chiral linkers catalytic sites can be incorporated directly into the bridging ligands used to construct MOFs, and is much more versatile for the immobilization of a variety of homogeneous catalysts. Template synthesis of chiral MOFs has received less attention until recently and has been utilized to synthesize several cation and anionic chiral frameworks. Ionic MOFs are of great importance for development of heterogeneous asymmetric catalysis by ionic catalysts and enantiomer separation by chiral cationor anion-exchange. Tagging MOFs enables their modification after synthesis, making it possible to convert them into novel chiral materials or fine-tune their properties and will for sure make a unique contribution to enantioselective processes. The integration of the three techniques is expected to provide, at least in principle, the widest combination of tools for chiral MOF fabrication
The developments in the introduction of porosity, rigidity and functionality in chiral MOFs demonstrates how the application of modern chemical design and synthetic methodologies can confer vitality on an area that had been assumed unpromising, in this case due to the intrinsic weakness of the framework-forming bonds and the resulting poor prospects for framework stability. For example, by using robust high-branched polydentate ligands, framework interpenetration that, in general, increase framework stability and limits pore size could be suppressed or control to increase pore size and porosity without concomitant sacrifice of the stability in some chiral MOFs. The combination of rigidity and flexibility into homochiral MOFs can impart a stable framework displaying dual properties of permanent porosity and guest-dependent dynamic behavior and may be realized by careful control of coordination bonds and weak interactions used to assemble components. Of further interest is that flexible MOFs can be related to biological systems if they are formed by metal-directed assembled from bioactive organic ligands or flexible organic linkers with rich hydrophobic and/or hydrophilic functionalities. Such bioanalogous MOFs have shown new and versatile guest recognition, catalysis and separation applications because of their better recognition and structural transformation abilities than those of rigid porous materials.
Although inferior to zeolites in thermal or hydrolytic stability, MOFs possess extensive tunability, highly regular catalytic sites, flexibility and homochirality, and have achieved some notable success in enantioselective processes, domains in which zeo
lites have met limited success. The facile tunability of such a molecular building block approach allows for precise and systematic engineering of chiral functionalities and active sites within MOFs. Some chiral porous MOFs have demonstrated high efficiency and/or excellent selectivity in some enantioselective catalytic or resolution processes and can be readily separated and reused in multiple cycles without significantly loss of activity and enantioselectivity. Chiral framework confinement was observed in some cases, which imparts substrate size selectivity, enhances catalyst or recognition activity and even increase or induce product enantioselectivity. The well-isolated active sites in MOFs would not only facilitate the structural elucidation and mechanistic study of their functions but also may lead to development of new porous materials for highly enantioselective processes. In particularly, for chiral separation applications, it is important to consider not only porosity and functions but also the flexibility of materials, because their enantioselective behaviors are mainly determined by subtle steric interactions of guest molecules with channel surfaces. The successful tailoring of adsorption and diffusion properties will thus be crucial and so chiral porous channels with the appropriate hydrophilic, hydrophobic or amphiphilic functionalities will need to be created for diverse substrates. In this case, molecular simulation is a powerful tool to predict enantioselective adsorption and may help gain insight into the corresponding molecular level phenomena.[107,108]
Overall, homochiral MOFs represent a key emerging field and many new opportunities exist for both exploratory and use-oriented research. One can imagine more functional homochiral MOF materials, particularly those involve "privileged" chiral organic ligands, chiral organic catalysts and bioactive molecules could be expected in the near future. Taking the current promise through to real-life applications will be an important goal over the next decade or so.
